an iconic aircraft previously consigned  to the great scrapyard in the sky  is now making a triumphant return  it's back the airbus a380 super jumble  is taking to the skies again thanks to a  resurgence in air traffic the world's  biggest passenger aircraft has been  pulled out of retirement by many  airlines including emirates and british  airways and for many the return of their  entire a380 fleets can't happen fast  enough  dubai-based emirates has by far the  world's biggest a380 fleet followed by  singapore airlines in third place comes  lufthansa even the germans are planning  to deploy some of their super jumbo  planes again  australia's qantas and british airways  round up the top five list for most a380  aircraft  lufthansa parked many aircraft in spain  when the covet 19 pandemic ground air  traffic to a halt the a380 was supposed  to be retired for good fought too big  unprofitable and bad for the planet but  times have clearly changed with  lufthansa now planning a380 voyages for  summer 2023  let's speak to aviation journalist  andreas spear to a little get a little  bit more about this because um it seems  a bit strange to me andreas the problems  with the a380 haven't gone away it's  still got those four big engines that  need lots and lots of fuel and it's  still massive it's still difficult to  fill as difficult as it was before the  pandemic so uh  why are airlines willing to look past  those problems and reinstate it  i think they're just in urgent need of  capacity and that as fast as possible so  the a380 fleet that was already kind of  mothballed and long-term storage around  europe and elsewhere where the easiest  solution and as you just rightly said  there's a huge surge of demand right now  and it's also foreseeable that that will  last until the next season starts so  actually as it takes quite some time to  reactivate them they'll just be ready in  time for the early summer season meaning  spring 2023.  yes as you mentioned it's it's not an  immediate thing it's not really going to  offer any relief to the problems that  we're seeing right now at airports is it  but why does it take so long i mean i  think lufthansa is not expecting to  bring them back in like you say until  the middle of next year  yes it takes a full nine months  lufthansa ceo carson told me recently to  reactivate an a380 from  so-called deep storage and deep storage  means the aircraft is kept in a  air-worthy condition on the ground but  still it takes a long time to kind of  get it run engines run up and kind of  get really the technical details and  checks being done as a huge aircraft  after all on the other hand also there's  a pilot shortage lufthansa has just kept  14 a380 captains current and that's the  reason the allied now will base its  newly revamped a380s from in munich  airport because that's where the a350s  the more modern aircraft is based and  there their pilots the a350 pilots can  easily be retrained and cross-trained to  also simultaneously fly the a380 that's  the reason for pilots and reactivation  in technical terms  so there's a clearly renewed uh demand  for for a large aircraft like this but  airbus isn't making them anymore and the  the jumbo jet the 747s also not being  made anymore so  where are they supposed to get these big  enough aircraft if they need them the  airlines  yeah that's a very big concern of some  islands especially emirates and  yesterday at the farmer air show in  england there was a high-level  panel with the head of yata the yata is  the uh  schedule association and sir tim clark  the emirates president and they both  agreed and it's really a big omission  it's almost like a sin uh not to look at  all at the moment  what the next generation in big aircraft  could be  boeing currently has problems delivering  on its largest aircraft that's in  production right now the triple 7-9 and  that's also one of the reasons the a380  is coming back as a stop-gap measure but  it's really a big fault of the industry  emirates says at least that nobody right  now looks at any big replacement like in  the midterm to long-term future  wow  nothing in the pipeline andrea spade  thanks a lot for joining us  thank you very much
 

